module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_control_that_shares_its_derivation_with_its_subject

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BlockerClassUnstated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data a_control_that_shares_its_derivation_with_its_subject: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_control_that_shares_its_derivation_with_its_subject" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: BlockerClassUnstated },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a control whose content is DERIVED FROM the thing it is meant to discriminate, so it cannot disagree with it. The general form is one sentence -- A CONTROL WRITTEN FROM THE IMPLEMENTATION CAN ONLY AGREE WITH IT -- and the two routes to it look different enough that neither author recognised the other's until they were put side by side. DESCRIBING: read what the code does, write the assertion that says so. COPYING: inline the production body into the control with one input swapped. Copying is simply the most literal way of writing a control from the implementation.",

    "HARM: the suite reports coverage where it has none, and reports it most confidently exactly where the subject is most load-bearing, because a heavily-relied-on fold is the one an author is most likely to describe accurately rather than interrogate. The mutation that removes the wall leaves the control green, so the wall's absence is invisible to every later reader -- and the control is then CITED as the reason the wall is safe to lean on.",

    "SPECIMEN ONE, DESCRIBING (gunbc#11962, review 69784, 2026-09-21). A witness over the /proc/meminfo parse asserted count(all_metrics) == 8 against a fixture whose eighth line is `HugePages_Total: 0` -- a unitless page count the parse was minting as a Kibibyte. The control did not catch the defect; it RECORDED it, because it was written by reading the fold and stating the result. The same change carried a second instance: a termination control that asserted completed-plus-unknown frees a seat, which was the defect the four-state predicate existed to remove.",

    "SPECIMEN TWO, COPYING (gunbc#12017, review 69791, 2026-09-21, session loyal-swift-608). a_refused_identity_refuses_the_store -- a RED written to satisfy a previous review -- carried the production door's body INLINED with only the verdict source swapped, so it evaluated its own copy of the green test it existed to guard. Deleting that test from the real door would have left the control green. The annotation above it asserted the opposite in as many words.",

    "SPECIMEN THREE, EXECUTED AND EXPENSIVE (srv1, 2026-11-09, 12.5 minutes of a serving replica). A witness over a rendered container command claimed the command WAS THE CAPTURED COMMAND and its test name said `byte for byte`. What it actually asked was whether each captured line appeared INSIDE the corresponding rendered line. The render had glued an input refusal onto the end of a captured shell line, so the rendered line was the captured line plus one extra token -- an unmatched `)` that made the container's bash exit 2 within a second, and the replica did not come back until the unit exhausted its restart burst. This is not a copied body and not a mis-transcribed fixture: the captured bytes in the module were correct, and the render differed from them. What made the control self-confirming was the STRENGTH of the comparison -- a containment test on a line is satisfied by that line plus anything -- so the control's verdict was derived from the same loose reading of the line that produced the render. The repair is a comparison that can disagree: the captured line must occur as a WHOLE line of the render and the START line must be unique in the script; and gates that do not travel through a representation this module authored, namely parsing the emitted bytes with the serving image's own bash and dry-running the rendered engine argv against the command the container actually ran. Generalizable form: A CONTROL THAT ASKS WHETHER ITS SUBJECT CONTAINS WHAT IT SHOULD HAS THE SAME WEAKNESS AS ONE WRITTEN FROM ITS SUBJECT -- containment cannot see a suffix, and a suffix is how concatenation defects arrive.",

    "SPECIMEN FOUR, A GOLDEN TRANSCRIBED FROM ITS OWN SUBJECT (gunbc#16248, review 93224, 2026-11-10). A positive serialization control asserted that a for loop renders as `for i in 1 2 do echo up; done` -- and bash REFUSES that string: `syntax error near unexpected token 'done'`, exit 2. The Bash manual, node \"Looping Constructs\", gives the form as `for name [ [in [words ...] ] ; ] do commands; done`: the word list must be terminated before 'do' just as the command list is terminated before 'done'. The lane carried the closing terminator ('; done') and dropped the opening one. The control was GREEN, and it had been written by reading what the builder printed. Builder and witness then formed a closed loop with no external referent: the only oracle in the room agreed with the subject by construction, and the defect was shipped with a passing suite attesting to it. THE TELL IS IN THE CONTROL, NOT THE CODE -- a golden made of nothing but the output of the function the control is about. THE REPAIR IS AN ORACLE THAT IS NOT THE MODEL: re-derive each expected string from the upstream documentation that governs its syntax, then hand every expected string to the real interpreter in syntax-only mode (`bash -n`) inside the same dispatch, so anything the language refuses goes red irrespective of what the builder emitted. An accept/reject gate the SUBSTRATE owns cannot have been written from the subject, which makes it the cheapest cure for this class wherever a real interpreter exists. ",

    "SPECIMEN FIVE, A DIGEST CHECKED AGAINST ITSELF (gunbc#16270, srv1 boot path, 2026-11-10/11). `the_install_digests_are_the_digests_of_the_render` asserted that `sha256_hex_of_text` of the launcher render equals `srv1_render_replica_launcher_sha256()` -- and that function IS `sha256_hex_of_text(text: srv1_render_replica_launcher())`, the same derivation called twice with nothing host-side in either arm. It could not fail. It was written by reading the two helper names and stating that they agree, and it was CITED -- in a worker brief, as the acceptance check for the whole migration -- which is the harm this class exists to describe: the suite looked like it held the installed bytes to account with two controls and it held them with one. THE ASK THAT CATCHES IT WITHOUT A RUN: name the state this control exists to catch (an installed file that is NOT the render) and notice that the control never reads a byte that ever left the module. The real comparison already existed one screen away -- `the_install_and_run_receipts_hold_their_own_claim`, through `install_row_digest_matches`, which compares the digest computed here against the `installed_sha256_computed_on_host` rows the host itself read back with `sha256sum` -- and it is that control which went RED when the container-command lines moved, while the tautology stayed green beside it. REPAIR: the tautology is deleted from the witness. Nothing real was lost with it -- the shape it pretended to check (a digest is 64 lowercase hex, and the installed bytes hash to it) is checked where the value is an INPUT: the receipts control puts lower_hex_64 on each artifact row and compares it to what the host read back. THE TELL, GENERALISED: a digest, a hash or a serialized form appearing on BOTH sides of an equality, with the same function on both.",

    "THE SMELL THAT NEEDS NO RUN, and it is what makes this cheaper to catch than to prove: ASK WHAT THE CONTROL SHARES WITH ITS SUBJECT. If it re-derives, re-implements or re-describes the thing under test, it is suspect before any mutation is attempted. Specimen two shared a copied body; specimen one shared a description. The mutation is the proof; the shared-derivation question is the smell that says to go looking.",

    "AND THE REPAIR HAS ITS OWN FAILURE MODE, WHICH IS THE MORE TRANSFERABLE HALF. The first fix of specimen one added the missing unit comparison -- and mutating that comparison away left the control STILL GREEN. A unitless line is excluded by the ARITY requirement rather than by the comparison, and /proc/meminfo publishes no wrong-unit line at all, so the comparison had no instance in the fixture population to discriminate and was a permanently green decoration (gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder: worse than absent, because it is cited as coverage). THE FIXTURE POPULATION HAS TO CONTAIN THE CASE THE CHECK EXISTS FOR -- and a REAL-WORLD CORPUS IS EXACTLY WHERE THAT CASE TENDS NOT TO OCCUR, which is why the fixture is where it must be authored. Adding `SomeFutureField: 64 MB` is what made the mutation fail.",

    "NOT predicate_vacuously_true_on_an_empty_domain, and the boundary matters because the remedy differs. There the domain is EMPTY and the check answers true about nothing. Here the domain is POPULATED and healthy-looking -- seven real metrics -- and merely lacks the one case the check discriminates. An emptiness guard does not catch it; only asking what case this check exists for, and whether the fixture contains it, does.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable, by the two habits above -- run the mutation before reporting a control, and ask what the control shares with its subject. Nothing refuses a control that agrees with its subject, because agreement is what a passing control looks like.",

    "CEILING: mechanically preventable. A mutation pass that rebinds each control's subject to a wrong arm and requires the control to red is decidable over the corpus, and a control that survives every mutation of its own subject is reportable as establishing nothing.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY: every control is executed against a mutated subject and must red, with a control that survives all mutations of its subject reported as non-discriminating. Deliberately not 'review controls for copied bodies' -- specimen one had no copied body and would pass that review unchanged.",

    "CONSUMPTION: the derived gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.roster recurring_failure_mode_roster includes this per-class declaration.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "extdeps.linux.proc_meminfo", decl_name: "meminfo_line_metric"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.os_proc_meminfo_witness", decl_name: "parsing_meminfo_reads_the_named_fields_and_refuses_a_missing_one"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.compute.work_provider_local", decl_name: "unit_termination_decide"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.srv1_serving_replica_layout", decl_name: "srv1_replica_container_command_lines"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.serving.srv1_replica_rendered_boot_path_witness", decl_name: "the_container_command_head_is_the_captured_command"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.srv1_serving_replica_layout", decl_name: "srv1_render_replica_launcher_sha256"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.serving.srv1_replica_rendered_boot_path_witness", decl_name: "install_row_digest_matches"),
  ],
}
